\SweaveOpts{concordance=TRUE}

\maketitle

We spend considerable effort avoiding disagreements with the people around us. We steer conversations away from contentious topics, soften our opinions, and sometimes remain silent rather than risk a fight. Yet, the conversations we are motivated to avoid also provide unique opportunities to connect with an understand others with opposing viewpoints and to critically reflect on our attitudes and behavior. When the conversation topic is societally-relevant (e.g., topics related to health, politics, or the environment), disagreements can have profound implications for individuals and, in aggregate, societal well-being and social cohesion; and when the conversation partner is someone we know - a friend, family member, colleague, or acquaintance - the encounter combines counter-attitudinal information with a socially meaningful source of influence that is more difficult to dismiss than a stranger or institution. In two pre-registered studies, we seek to enhance our understanding of the circumstances that predict the occurrence and outcomes of societally-relevant disagreements with known others (DKOs).

Despite their potential impact, the occurrence and outcomes of societally-relevant DKOs remain hard to predict for social scientists and individuals alike. Researchers have reported both successes and profound failures of attempts to intentionally use interpersonal communication to obtain desired outcomes like attitude change or decreased polarization \parencite{DAVID, META_ANALYSIS}. Survey research has uncovered widespread misperceptions, for instance about how enjoyable a disagreement will be and about how open those with opposite opinions might be to new arguments. Here, we systematically attempt to predict three important dimensions: First, the general dislike for and low frequency of societally DKOs makes it crucial to predict when they do occur. Second, the strength of the impact of a given DKO and our likelihood to engage in similar conversations in the future partially depends on whether we perceive it to be valuable for ourselves (e.g., perceived enjoyment) and our social relationships (valuation outcomes). Finally, the significance of DKOs for individual and societal well-being depends on their propensity to change attitudes and behavior (change outcoems).

Existing evidence predicting occurrence, valuation, and change outcomes of diverse interpersonal interactions suggests a tension whereby predictors of one dimension may be counterproductive for another. For instance, while negative emotionality is linked to the occurrence of interpersonal interactions (BERGER), it is also associated with low enjoyment and relational harm; and while motives to persuade the conversation partner may enhance change goals, they could cause resistance, relational harm, and limit enjoyment. Nevertheless, this impression is built on evidence from disconnected strands of research: observational work examines when and with whom people discuss contentious topics, highlighting the dominance of DKOs over conversations with strangers (CITE), whereas experimental work examines how conversational characteristics and motives affect change and valuation outcomes, often in artificial interactions between strangers or under experimentally specified conversational goals. Most existing studies are further confined to specific topic areas. 

In Study 1, we test theory-driven, pre-registered hypotheses about the act of planning a DKO as predictor of DKO occurrence and change outcomes, and intellectual humility as a predictor of discourse quality and, ultimately, valuation outcomes of DKOs about societally-relevant topics including health, politics, and the environment in a large, representative sample of the Dutch population. In Study 2, we develop interventions encouraging planning and exhibiting intellectual humility during societally-relevant DKOs and test their causal, longitudinal impact on real-world interactions. This work estimates and attempts to enhance the predictability of societally-relevant DKOs and their outcomes as an individually and societally meaningful mechanism towards social cohesion and change.

\subsection{Theoretical Background Study 1}

On average, the existing literature supports a bleak image of disagreements. Wald, Kardas, and Epley \citeyear{waldMisplacedDividesDiscussing2024} found across three experiments that people systematically underestimate how positive disagreements about divisive topics will be. Experimental studies of conversations between strangers generally confirm this mismatch, with actual experiences rated more positively than expected \parencite{binnquistZoomSolutionPromoting2022}.

Several rich research traditions have advanced our knowledge of the nature and effects of interpersonal conversations in general, and interpersonal conflict or disagreement, specifically.  Whether the same holds for interactions with known others is less clear: relational stakes are higher, but so is familiarity, and expectations may be better calibrated to prior experience. Importantly, outcomes need not be uniform across dimensions.

H1: On average, DKOs are perceived as unenjoyable (H1A), are unlikely to change attitudes (H1B) and behavior (H1C), and do not motivate repetition of similar conversations (H1D).; and
H2: The majority of societally-relevant DKOs occurs between known others rather than strangers. 

Yet our ability to make generalizable predictions about the occurrence and outcomes of societally-relevant DKOs remains severely limited, partially due to two sharp dividing lines in the existing literature. 

First, research on the frequency of DKOs is largely disconnected from work on the characteristics and outcomes of the conversations. Frequency is typically studied using observational methods which highlight a preference for engaging with known others. Work on characteristics and outcomes tends to be experimental and favor controlled interactions between strangers. This disconnect complicates both the construction of a holistic understanding of DKOs and the safe design of interventions: increasing frequency without knowing likely outcomes can easily backfire, and costly outcome-focused interventions are wasted if the conversations they target rarely occur. The focus of experimental work on stranger interactions further raises questions about the generalizability of findings to interactions with known others which have more complex and potentially more consequential social contexts and implications. People spontaneously represent and incorporate the responses of socially close others into their own judgments and behaviors \parencite{smithInfluenceRepresentationsOthers2016}.  De Jong \citeyear{dejongCrosspartisanDiscussionsReduced2024} showed that brief cross-partisan conversations increased out-party sympathy and willingness to engage in future discussions without changing core policy positions, with the sympathy effect attenuated when issue disagreement was greater. 
 Together, these findings underscore that relational and attitudinal outcomes can dissociate, and that both must be measured and targeted.

Second, empirical data are siloed within disconnected topic areas - marital conflict \parencite{kanterDoesCoupleCommunication2022}, organizational knowledge exchange \parencite{vanwoerkomRomanceLearningDisagreement2010}, cross-partisan political conversations \parencite{combsReducingPoliticalPolarization2023a}, health communication \parencite{hendriksWhatDutchCollege2015}, and environmental peer persuasion \parencite{makiEnvironmentalPeerPersuasion2017}-making individual findings difficult to generalize or compare across contexts. 

Here we review this disconnected literature to derive theory-driven hypotheses about predictors of our three key outcome dimensions and then test these predictions using data on DKOs across three societally-relevant topics. Specifically, we focus on a pair of predictors that could potentially maximize all outcome dimensions without counteracting one another: Planning and intellectual humility. 

\subsubsection{Planning as a Predictor of Occurrence}
COMMENT: THERE IS A KEY ISSUE HERE THA TWE INCLUDED A LOT MORE HYPOTHESES. THERE WERE HYPOTHESES THAT ARE SIMPLY OUT OF SCOPE, BUT WE DID ALSO HYPOTHESIZE THAT PLANNING WOULD INCREASE DISCOURSE QUALITY, WHICH IT DIDN'T. MY CURRENT SOLUTION IS TO NOT DISCUSS IT HERE FOR THE SAKE OF CLARITY AND BREVITY, BUT I DO WANT TO BE TRANSPARENT ABOUT IT. I'M CONSIDERING TO INCLUDE AN ONLINE RESOURCE THAT TESTS ALL PRE-REGISTERED HYPOTHESES AND TO INCLUDE AN ADDITIONAL PARAGRAPH THERE FOR ALL HYPOTHESES THAT ARE IN SOME SORT OF CONTRAST WITH THE THEORY WE WRITE IN THE PAPER. 

By planning, we mean a concrete and detailed plan to engage in a specific societally-relevant DKO with a specific known other at a specific time. We theorize that planning a DKO enhances frequency while essentially substituting naturally occurring frequency predictors that have been linked to counterproductive DKOs.

Prior observational work suggests that interpersonal conversations about societally-relevant topics like health, politics, and especially about the environment are relatively infrequent \parencite{atariPaucityMoralityEveryday2023, keskinturkWhatAreYou2025}. For instance, Latkin and colleauges \citeyear{latkinTheyTalkWeather2024} observed that over 60\% of their large sample had not discussed climate change with friends or family in the previous month. The frequency and nature of disagreements is rarely studied and most work is concentrated in the domain of politics. The existing evidence highlights that societally-relevant disagreements occur infrequently and, overwhelmingly, with known others rather than strangers \parencite{hofstraImpactDyadsExtended2026, makiEnvironmentalPeerPersuasion2017, jostCognitiveMotivationalMechanisms2022, hendriksWhatDutchCollege2015, moreyWhoMattersTypes2012, schmitt-beckEverydayPoliticalTalk2023}. For instance, the Survey Center on American Life \parencite{soal} found that 58\% of US Americans disagreed about politics with a friend or family member in the previous year while only 34\% had done so with a stranger. 

While the specific predictors of societally-relevant DKOs remain unknown, social psychologists and communication scientists have established a variety of circumstances that increase the likelihood of social interactions. Among those are various dimensions of self-relevance, social relevance, and (especially negative) emotionality (CITES). This general theory fits with the sparse existing data. For instance, Schmitt-Beck and Schnaudt \citeyear{schmitt-beckEverydayPoliticalTalk2023} identify conflict-seeking disposition and political topic interest as positive predictors of political disagreements, highlighting that people who on aerage find political topics more self-relevant and who perceive lower social costs of disagreements are more likely to engage. 

The natural predictors of interactions create a problematic situation, because topics that are not discussed often may be perceived as low in social relevance, humans have a natural tendency to avoid counter-attitudinal content (CITE XINYAO AS SUMBITTED and MEZULIS) and disagreements in particulary since they are often perceived to be negative, are less likely to be perceived as potentially rewarding for the self and one's social relationships. Being highly emotional about a topic and wanting to express or regulate that emotion socially may overwrite the self- and social hesitations. However, disagreements started in the context of strong negative emotions may lead to resistance, low enjoyment and relational harm. In other words, natural predictors of social interactions make disagreements unlikely and if they do occur might make them counterproductive. 

Planning a disagreement might circumvent this dynamic. Related work on implementation intentions suggests that concrete plans to engage in a certain behavior can enhance the likelihood of that behavior occurring. Further, work on sharing suggests that when given the opportunity and time to consider potential routes through which content could be framed as socially or self-relevant enhances people's likelihood to engage in social interactions about content even if they wouldn't have done so otherwise. As such, planning can reduce the perceived self- and social relevance barriers while increasing DKO frequency without relying on the crutch of emotions. 

HYPOTHESIS

\subsubsection{Planning as a Predictor of Change}

There is a strong theoretical basis for expecting interactions with close others to be impactful: work on the RICOR model \parencite{smithInfluenceRepresentationsOthers2016} proposes that responses from psychologically close others are spontaneously represented and incorporated into one's own judgments, providing an automatic mechanism for social influence that does not depend on deliberate conformity motives. Longitudinal political-network research corroborates this, showing tha tdisagreement and partner selection are intertwined and that friends and family exert meaningufl influence on political attitudes over time \parencite{belloInfluenceMightierSelection2014}. Health psychology and communication scholars have similarly demonstrated the influence of naturally occurring interpersonal conversations on health and environmental behaviors \parencite{hendriksInterveningConversationsHow2020, makiEnvironmentalPeerPersuasion2017}, though disagreements specifically remain understudied. 

Relatively little is known about what predicts the outcomes of naturally occurring disagreements. The existing experimental literature is almost exclusively focused on politics and on two levers: conversation quality and conversational motives. 

Prior work highlights the strength of social influence exerted in interpersonal communication, showing that conversations can impact health behaviors, political participation, and environmental behaviors. Yet, we know little about the effects of disagreements specifically on attitude and behavior change. 

Emotions increase the probability of initiatying a conversation but may impair careful listening and promote motivated reasoning. Social cohesion motives drive interaction, but DKOs require a tolerance for conflict that cohesion motives may actively suppress.

When people disagree with known others, they can draw on a shared history and a deep understanding of the relationship. A planned conversation allows them to leverage this knowledge to calibrate their approach; an emotionally charged, unplanned onset is likely to undermine it. We therefore hypothesize:

H11: Planned vs. unplanned DKOs are perceived to be more instructive (H2A) more enjoyable (H2B), less likely to harm the relationship of the conversation partners (H2C), more likely to lead to attitude (H2D) and behavior change (H2E), and more likely to be repeated (H2F). 

\subsubsectio{Intellectual Humility as a Predictor of Valuation}
Contact theory suggests that exposure to members of opposing groups leads to greater social closeness



Combs and colleagues \citeyear{combsReducingPoliticalPolarization2023a} similarly found that actual cross-party conversations produced substantial reductions in affective polarization, with reductions tied to the civility of the interaction. 

H8: Higher intellectual humility is associated with higher discourse quality during DKOs. 

De Jong \citeyear{dejongCrosspartisanDiscussionsReduced2024} identified perspective-taking, feelings of inclusion, perceived commonality, and reduced misperceptions as predictors of conversational outcomes. Similarly, Speer and colleagues \citeyear{speerFindingAgreementFunctional2026} found that exploration and openness during conversations predicted agreement. Santoro and colleagues \citeyear{santoroListenChangeLongitudinal2025} found that high-quality, non-judgmental listening increased interpersonal perceptions and reduced defensiveness. DeMarree and colleagues \citeyear{demarreeLearningGoalsListening2026} found that a goal to listen to a disagreeing stranger rather than to persuade increased perceived psychological safety and, thereby, reduced polarization. These findings converge on the conclusion that higher discourse quality consistently predicts better relational and experiential outcomes, even when attitudinal change is absent: Nussbaum \citeyear{nussbaumEffectGoalInstructions2005} found that a persuasion goal generates more adversarial communication than an exploration goal. Speer and colleagues \citeyear{speerFindingAgreementFunctional2026} found that a compromise goal produced more agreement than a persuasion goal. Santoro and colleagues \citeyear{santoroListenChangeLongitudinal2025} manipulated high-quality listening in trained canvassers showing that listening improved interpersonal outcomes, but not persuasion. Together, these findings suggest that conversational motives are important, but have only examined the role of experimentally manipulated motives in pairs focusing on conversations with strangers. The effects of naturally occurring motives in DKOs reamin an open question we address empirically.

H10: DKOs with higher discourse quality are perceived to be more instructive (H1A), more enjoyable (H1B), less likely to harm the relationship of the conversation partners (H1C) and associated with higher willingness to have similar conversations in the future (H1D). 

