@

After providing informed consent (ERB # ANONYMIZED), participants completed an online survey consisting of two main parts: In Part 1, participants were asked to recall details about one specific DKO. Participants were randomly assigned to one of the three focal topics (health, politics, or the environment). Participants who were not able to recall a DKO about the assigned topic where first asked to recall a DKO about another focal topic. Participants who did not recall any societally-relevant DKO, were asked to recall a DKO about any topic. In Part 2, participants were asked to report experiences with and impressions of societally-relevant DKOs in general. The pre-registration, measures, and analysis scripts are available on our OSF page. 

\subsection{Participants}

We recruited Dutch-speaking participants via the panel company Dynata, based on quotas representing the Dutch population in terms of age, gender, and education (see details in supplementary materials). $\Sexpr{sum(allS1$completed==1)}$ completed the survey. In accordance with our pre-registration, we excluded participants who failed two out of three attention checks (N=$\Sexpr{sum(allS1$completed==1 & allS1$attentionPassed==0)}$), those who completed the survey implausibly fast (N=$\Sexpr{sum(allS1$completed==1 & allS1$attentionPassed==1 & allS1$time_exclusion==1)}$) or unusually fast with a high percentage of straightlined answers (see analysis scrips for details; N=$\Sexpr{sum(allS1$completed==1 & allS1$attentionPassed==1 & allS1$time_straightliner_exclusion==1)}$). A final sample of $\Sexpr{sum(allS1$completed==1 & allS1$attentionPassed==1 & allS1$time_exclusion==0 & allS1$time_straightliner_exclusion==0)}$ participants (Mage=$\Sexpr{round(mean(S1$age_1),2)}$, SDage=$\Sexpr{round(sd(S1$age_1),2)}$) were included in the analysis. $\Sexpr{sum(S1$gender==1)}$ participants reported their gender identity as female, $\Sexpr{sum(S1$gender==2)}$ as male, $\Sexpr{sum(S1$gender==3)}$ as non-binary, and $\Sexpr{sum(S1$gender==4)}$ as other. The sample is educationally (N=$\Sexpr{sum(S1$Education==1)}$ no formal education or elementary school, N=$\Sexpr{sum(S1$Education==2)}$ Vmbo, havo/vwo-onderbouw, mbo1, N=$\Sexpr{sum(S1$Education==3)}$ Havo, VWO, mbo2-4, N=$\Sexpr{sum(S1$Education==4)}$ Hbo/vwo-bachelor, N=$\Sexpr{sum(S1$Education==5)}$ Hbo/vwo-master or doctorate) and politically balanced, but diverse (M=$\Sexpr{round(mean(S1$political_leaning,na.rm=T),2)}$, SD=$\Sexpr{round(sd(S1$political_leaning,na.rm=T),2)}$ on a scale from 0=politically left to 10=politically right).

Of these participants, we excluded an additional $\Sexpr{sum(S1$conv_topic_FLAG==1,na.rm=T)}$ datapoints from analyses of specific recalled societally-relevant DKOs for not naming an interpretable specific topic within the target category and $\Sexpr{sum(S1$conv_topic==4,na.rm=T)}$ observations for recalling a DKO about a non-focal topic.

\subsection{Measures}

Justification of measures
propensity of following certain motives and the baseline conversation quality across societally-relevant topics in DKOs as a backdrop that highlights specific needs and opportunities for interventions. Exisiting research has particularly focused on the distinction between persuasion and non-persuasive motives like compromising (SPEER ET AL PRE-PRINT). From other work on the motives that drive social interactions, we also know that people engage to improve their social relationships and to learn new things. In terms of conversation quality, a well-defined variable is perceived discourse quality which can be extended wtih enjoyment an indicator of perceived reward and utility. 

\subsection{Analysis}


\section{Results}

\subsection{Frequency}
Overall, the GENERAL part of the survey suggests that disagreements are relatively infrequent (@fig-frequency). The most common reported frequency of "any disagreement" was "2-11x/year".

With regards to the three focal topics, conversations about the environment were least frequent, with the most common response being "2-11x/year", closely followed by "less than yearly". Conversations about health were somewhat more frequent ("2-11x/year" and "monthly" being the most common categories). Conversations about politics were most frequent, with the most common response being "2-11x/year", closely followed by "weekly" and "monthly".

Disagreements about the focal topics were even less frequent with the most likely responses for the environment and health being "less than yearly" and the most likely response for politics being "2-11x/year".

